## Before you start (in Flow)

1. Open flow.google.com with the channel account. In **Agent settings**, set video to **9:16** and **x1** (x2 makes two clips and costs double).
2. Choose the model **{{ flow_model }}**.
{% if brief %}3. **Check image first (0 credits, recommended):** for each shot, make its check image (*Images* below, Flow
   image mode, 9:16), save it as `image_NN.png` and run `uv run factory clips review {{ item.id }}`; regenerate (free)
   until it passes. **Then the video (20 credits):** **Frames to video** with that image (or plain text to video),
   paste the shot's *Video* prompt, generate with {{ flow_model }}, download it, then
   `uv run factory clips review {{ item.id }} --shots NN`.
{% else %}3. **Images first (0 credits):** make every image in *Images* below (Flow image mode, 9:16), save each as
   `image_NN.png` in the inbox and check them: `uv run factory clips review {{ item.id }}`. Images are free, so
   generate again until each one passes. Shots share images: the end image of one shot is the start image of the next.
   **Then each video (20 credits):** **Frames to video** with the shot's start image (and its end image, if Flow
   offers an end-frame slot), paste the shot's *Video* prompt, generate with {{ flow_model }}, download it, then
   `uv run factory clips review {{ item.id }} --shots NN` checks direction, motion and blur.
{% endif %}4. Save each download with exactly this name into the inbox folder:
   `{{ inbox_windows }}\shot_NN.mp4` (NN = the shot number below, e.g. `shot_02.mp4`).
5. Log the credits you spent: `uv run factory flow-log --credits <N>`
6. Run `uv run factory generate` again. It moves the clips into the project once all of them are there.

Flow makes {{ clip_seconds }}-second clips; the edit uses the first seconds of each, as listed.

